    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters June 15 with a clear hierarchy of efficiency, led by the elite output of **Corbin Carroll** (85.71) and **James Wood** (76.28). **Corbin Carroll** is our primary engine today; facing Ure\u00f1a, he benefits from a premier lineup spot (#2) and the aggressive base-running environment we prioritize, helping him overcome a minor penalty for the opposing catcher\u2019s elite pop time. **James Wood** is equally dangerous, capitalizing on a soft matchup against Spence (xERA 6.03) with a high-floor profile anchored by a 0.403 xwOBA, making him our strongest play for both power and counting stats. **Gabriel Moreno** and **Seiya Suzuki** also provide strong utility; **Gabriel Moreno** gains significant leverage from his projected #3 spot against Ure\u00f1a, while **Seiya Suzuki** enjoys favorable wind conditions, adding +5% to his efficiency score against Lorenzen.\n\nOur roster depth remains tested as we navigate these lineups, but the decision to sit **Zach McKinstry** (43.13) was straightforward given his bottom-tier projection. The most difficult \"keep-out\" was **Kyle Karros** (49.66); despite benefiting from a platoon edge against Imanaga and favorable wind, his bottom-of-the-order placement (#8) and the overall difficulty of the opposing arm suppressed his score just below our utility threshold. We are monitoring the status of **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, whose scores are volatile due to pending lineup verification; if either slips further down the order, we risk efficiency decay. While stalwarts like **Josh Naylor** and **Jose Caballero** are sidelined by team off-days, we remain cautious regarding the health of **Mickey Moniak** and **Trevor Story**, who remain on the IL, limiting our ability to rotate in high-upside speed threats like **Brice Turang** or **Carson Williams**. Owners should keep a close eye on the weather report for the **Tyler Soderstrom** matchup against Jared Jones, as any shift in conditions could impact his park-adjusted projection.",
